    "root_cause": "DEBUG-level logging was enabled on the api-server host pool during an earlier troubleshooting session (~1 hour before) and never reverted. The elevated log volume filled the /var/log disk to 100%. With the disk full, api-server could not write its request log or create temp files ('No space left on device'), so a growing fraction of requests failed - tracking exactly the hosts that hit 100% disk. Recovery required reverting the log level and rotating the accumulated debug logs.",
    "evidence_path": [
      "api-server INFO ~1h before: log level set to DEBUG on the host pool for a troubleshooting session",
      "disk_used_pct on /var/log climbs from ~55% to 100%",
      "api-server ERROR: disk full, cannot write request log / create temp files (No space left on device)",
      "error_rate rises to ~22%, correlated with the hosts at 100% disk",
      "recovery after the log level is reverted to INFO and old debug logs are rotated"
    ],
    "fix": "Revert the log level and rotate the debug logs (done). Make elevated log levels time-boxed with automatic revert, alert on disk usage well before 100%, and keep application temp files off the log volume.",
